# Blockquote

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A blockquote sets apart words that someone else wrote or said, such as a line from an installer’s handover note. Use it for a real quotation, not to indent or highlight your own text. For a note that is not a quotation, use [Inset text](inset-text.html).

[Text formatting](../content/formatting-content/text-formatting.html) owns the writing rule: quote someone else at length only, and do not use italics.

This page covers:

- Blockquote
  - `<blockquote>`
    - `<cite>`
  - `<q>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A quotation has three parts: a line that says who is speaking, the quoted passage, and the source. Put the speaker before the quote or in a caption after it. A quote in a Home Assistant Markdown card has a vertical rule on its left, in the divider colour.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-blockquote"><code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code></h3>

A block of quoted text, which can hold paragraphs. Browsers indent it and add no quotation marks. The `cite` attribute can hold the address of the source, but most people never see it, so name the source in words.

For a credited quote, wrap it in a `<figure>` and put the source in a `<figcaption>` after it, as in the example.

In a Home Assistant Markdown card, write the quote with a `>` at the start of the line. Home Assistant draws it with a 4 pixel rule on the left, in the `divider-color` theme variable, with no left margin and a small gap of padding. The Civica theme sets `divider-color` to #CECECE in light mode and #50565A in dark mode. The rule width and padding are fixed. A dashboard author cannot change them with a theme variable.

A Markdown quote whose first line is an alert marker, such as `[!NOTE]`, is drawn as an alert instead of a quote. See [Alert & Tip Callout](alert-and-tip-callout.html).

Do not use a blockquote for a suggestion that Home Assistant has written for the person. It is not a quote.

<h3 id="child-blockquote--cite"><code>&lt;cite&gt;</code></h3>

Names the title of a work, such as a handover note or a manual. Do not use it for a person’s name. Browsers show it in italics, and the Civica text rules say not to use italics, so set `font-style: normal` on it, as the example does. It has no role of its own for a screen reader. It usually sits inside the `<figcaption>`.

<h3 id="child-q"><code>&lt;q&gt;</code></h3>

A short quote inside a sentence. The browser adds the quotation marks for the page language, so do not type your own. A few screen readers say "quote" before it. Use it only for words someone else said.

If a quote is in another language, set the `lang` attribute on it so a screen reader says it correctly.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A blockquote is a real structure, so it is better than indented text.
- Quotation marks and the left rule do not carry the meaning alone. The speaker is named in words.
- A Markdown card keeps `<figure>`, `<figcaption>`, `<cite>` and the `cite` attribute on `<blockquote>`, but does not allow `<q>` or `<time>`; they show as plain text. Use `<blockquote>` for quotes there, and write a short quote in a sentence with ordinary quotation marks.
- The rule width, padding and the italic style of `<cite>` are not set by the theme.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Say who is speaking before the quote. Keep the quote short and end it clearly. Do not use large decorative quotation marks. Do not use a blockquote for layout.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: use a blockquote for a quotation only.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the rule is not the only sign that this is a quote.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): quoted text uses the normal text colour.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the quote wraps and stays readable.
- 3.1.2 Language of Parts: mark a quote in another language.

